1
00:00:07,946 --> 00:00:10,573
He's a really unusual lawyer.

2
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:13,451
I would like to meet him.

3
00:00:13,535 --> 00:00:15,370
No, it's better if you don't.

4
00:00:15,453 --> 00:00:16,329
Really?

5
00:00:16,412 --> 00:00:18,790
He's the exact opposite of you,
Ms. Shiramoto.

6
00:00:18,873 --> 00:00:23,044
He lies as naturally as he breathes,
and you can never tell what he's thinking.

7
00:00:23,127 --> 00:00:25,255
Also, he does things
entirely at his own pace.

8
00:00:25,338 --> 00:00:28,091
He's definitely an only child,
no doubt about it.

9
00:00:28,675 --> 00:00:30,802
It's bad for a lawyer to be prejudiced.

10
00:00:30,885 --> 00:00:32,512
That's true, but…

11
00:00:34,055 --> 00:00:36,683
TEITO MEDICAL UNIVERSITY

12
00:00:36,766 --> 00:00:39,769
BEST THESIS AWARD CEREMONY

13
00:00:39,853 --> 00:00:43,606
Prof. Shimazu is the university's
first female recipient of this award.

14
00:00:43,690 --> 00:00:45,108
How do you feel right now?

15
00:00:46,276 --> 00:00:47,652
Currently, female professors

16
00:00:48,236 --> 00:00:51,906
make up less than 10%
of medical faculties in Japan.

17
00:00:52,532 --> 00:00:55,535
I feel that I was able to get
my research to this point

18
00:00:55,618 --> 00:00:58,496
only thanks to the university's support.

19
00:00:58,997 --> 00:01:04,586
I would be happy if this award
inspires young female researchers.

20
00:01:05,295 --> 00:01:06,546
I heard you're in talks

21
00:01:06,629 --> 00:01:09,632
with a drug company
regarding your project?

22
00:01:09,716 --> 00:01:13,178
NEUROLOGY DEPARTMENT
PROFESSOR MARIKO SHIMAZU

23
00:01:13,261 --> 00:01:14,262
About that…

24
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,186
Regarding that matter,

25
00:01:21,269 --> 00:01:25,231
several pharmaceutical companies
are currently showing high interest,

26
00:01:25,315 --> 00:01:27,901
and we are discussing
a licensing agreement.

27
00:01:27,984 --> 00:01:28,818
Prof. Shimazu…

28
00:01:28,902 --> 00:01:30,653
They're desperate to promote her.

29
00:01:31,321 --> 00:01:34,198
She's just a poster child
for the university.

30
00:01:34,282 --> 00:01:37,911
They care more about a female figurehead
than the actual research.

31
00:01:38,494 --> 00:01:41,289
To have my research highly…

32
00:01:41,372 --> 00:01:45,543
Prof. Oyamada probably isn't pleased,
being in the same neurology department.

33
00:01:45,627 --> 00:01:48,213
Their research topics overlapped too.

34
00:01:48,755 --> 00:01:53,259
Going forward, I will keep dedicating
myself as a researcher,

35
00:01:53,343 --> 00:01:55,637
so as not to bring shame
to the university's name.

36
00:02:00,892 --> 00:02:07,190
KIKUTA TRANSPORT

37
00:02:07,273 --> 00:02:10,193
OVERDUE PAYMENT

38
00:02:31,297 --> 00:02:34,425
YOUR DAUGHTER WAS SUPPOSED TO HAVE PASSED

39
00:02:38,763 --> 00:02:42,767
MEISHO MEDICAL UNIVERSITY
ENTRANCE CEREMONY

40
00:02:45,103 --> 00:02:49,023
I did it again. What am I going to do?

41
00:02:49,107 --> 00:02:50,608
Good morning.

42
00:02:50,692 --> 00:02:51,901
Good morning.

43
00:02:51,985 --> 00:02:54,112
-Good morning.
-What's wrong with him?

44
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:56,614
It's a chronic condition. Don't mind him.

45
00:02:56,698 --> 00:02:58,408
A chronic condition? Is he okay?

46
00:02:58,491 --> 00:03:00,076
It's a mild gambling addiction.

47
00:03:00,159 --> 00:03:03,579
He was supposed to pay his daughter's
high school tuition this month,

48
00:03:03,663 --> 00:03:04,914
but he lost it all.

49
00:03:04,998 --> 00:03:05,957
What?

50
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:08,543
I was sure it was a safe bet.

51
00:03:08,626 --> 00:03:10,670
You're the worst father.

52
00:03:10,753 --> 00:03:12,463
That's why your wife left you.

53
00:03:12,547 --> 00:03:14,507
That has nothing to do with gambling.

54
00:03:14,590 --> 00:03:17,760
On top of that, during the divorce,
he fought his wife over custody…

55
00:03:17,844 --> 00:03:19,095
Hey!

56
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:21,597
Don't go running your mouth.

57
00:03:23,016 --> 00:03:26,769
What kind of excuse
should I make to my ex-wife?

58
00:03:26,853 --> 00:03:28,438
Hey, you know any good lies?

59
00:03:28,521 --> 00:03:29,814
Lying is bad.

60
00:03:30,398 --> 00:03:32,525
-But…
-You should tell her the truth.

61
00:03:32,608 --> 00:03:35,611
I like how straightforward she is.

62
00:03:35,695 --> 00:03:38,031
Really? Dating someone like her is tough.

63
00:03:38,114 --> 00:03:39,407
I think so too.

64
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:40,491
What did you say?

65
00:03:40,575 --> 00:03:42,118
Nothing.

66
00:03:42,994 --> 00:03:46,331
I wonder if the weather will hold up.

67
00:03:46,414 --> 00:03:48,333
I'm going to a site in Hachiba now.

68
00:03:48,416 --> 00:03:49,792
<i>In other news…</i>

69
00:03:49,876 --> 00:03:51,836
<i>Regarding Teito Medical University's</i>
<i>entry exam,</i>

70
00:03:51,919 --> 00:03:54,339
<i>parents of an applicant</i>
<i>have filed a lawsuit</i>

71
00:03:54,422 --> 00:03:57,050
<i>against the university, seeking damages.</i>

72
00:03:57,133 --> 00:04:00,053
<i>I can't forgive the university</i>
<i>that stole my daughter's dream.</i>

73
00:04:00,636 --> 00:04:03,556
<i>In this year's</i>
<i>Teito Medical University entry exam,</i>

74
00:04:03,639 --> 00:04:06,726
<i>the scores of female applicants</i>
<i>were uniformly adjusted.</i>

75
00:04:06,809 --> 00:04:08,895
Didn't this happen before?

76
00:04:08,978 --> 00:04:10,563
Even in the Reiwa era…

77
00:04:10,646 --> 00:04:13,483
People and organizations
don't change that easily.

78
00:04:13,566 --> 00:04:15,234
-Are you talking about me?
-But…

79
00:04:15,318 --> 00:04:18,863
Treating someone unfairly because
she's a woman is truly unforgivable.

80
00:04:18,946 --> 00:04:20,698
Women always end up losing out.

81
00:04:20,782 --> 00:04:22,200
But aren't there also times

82
00:04:22,283 --> 00:04:24,577
when they benefit
just because they are women?

83
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:25,578
What?

84
00:04:25,661 --> 00:04:28,623
True, lately many companies
are saying things like

85
00:04:28,706 --> 00:04:31,084
"hiring women"
or "hiring female managers."

86
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:32,543
That's discrimination too.

87
00:04:32,627 --> 00:04:36,297
It's blatantly obvious that they just want
to use women's empowerment as PR.

88
00:04:36,381 --> 00:04:37,965
They're looking down on women.

89
00:04:38,549 --> 00:04:39,509
Blatant?

90
00:04:40,009 --> 00:04:41,469
I'm sorry, I just…

91
00:04:44,931 --> 00:04:45,765
Yes.

92
00:04:46,307 --> 00:04:47,850
<i>I need to ask you a favor.</i>

93
00:04:48,559 --> 00:04:50,144
<i>Get here now.</i>

94
00:04:50,228 --> 00:04:51,229
What?

95
00:05:04,033 --> 00:05:06,911
My apologies,
media presence inside the campus

96
00:05:06,994 --> 00:05:10,289
will disturb the students,
so please leave!

97
00:05:10,373 --> 00:05:15,211
We are currently verifying the facts,
so we cannot make any statements!

98
00:05:15,294 --> 00:05:18,089
You must be a journalist?
Please do not enter.

99
00:05:18,172 --> 00:05:19,090
Please leave!

100
00:05:19,173 --> 00:05:21,342
I'm a lawyer. I was called here.

101
00:05:21,426 --> 00:05:23,428
My apologies. Go ahead.

102
00:05:23,511 --> 00:05:24,804
Please leave.

103
00:05:25,596 --> 00:05:27,557
Stop filming!

104
00:05:29,308 --> 00:05:30,935
Mr. Uramawashi.

105
00:05:31,018 --> 00:05:34,814
The favor you mentioned, you don't mean
defending the university, right?

106
00:05:34,897 --> 00:05:36,691
How nostalgic.

107
00:05:36,774 --> 00:05:39,902
I graduated from this school.

108
00:05:40,528 --> 00:05:41,529
Really?

109
00:05:42,113 --> 00:05:43,990
You're an architect and a lawyer.

110
00:05:44,073 --> 00:05:47,660
And now you're a medical school graduate?
Stop it with the stupid lies.

111
00:05:47,743 --> 00:05:49,287
They're waiting. Let's hurry.

112
00:05:49,370 --> 00:05:52,248
Hey, are you listening to me?

113
00:05:52,331 --> 00:05:56,460
Our university has not engaged
in any exam fraud.

114
00:05:58,796 --> 00:06:03,092
Then, how do you explain this lawsuit?

115
00:06:03,176 --> 00:06:04,468
I don't know.

116
00:06:04,552 --> 00:06:08,306
However, every year,
failing applicants and their parents

117
00:06:08,389 --> 00:06:11,559
do send us inquiries
requesting score disclosures.

118
00:06:11,642 --> 00:06:12,935
That's a fact.

119
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:18,649
The one who filed the lawsuit,
Mr. Kikuta, do you know him?

120
00:06:18,733 --> 00:06:21,903
According to the admissions office,
he came to protest once.

121
00:06:21,986 --> 00:06:23,196
We told him back then

122
00:06:23,279 --> 00:06:27,950
that his daughter's results came
from a thorough evaluation by a committee.

123
00:06:28,868 --> 00:06:32,580
Could your university have unfairly
influenced the committee…

124
00:06:32,663 --> 00:06:33,915
That's impossible.

125
00:06:35,291 --> 00:06:38,294
Similar issues have occurred
at other universities.

126
00:06:38,377 --> 00:06:39,295
To prevent this,

127
00:06:39,378 --> 00:06:43,883
we selected a paragon of honesty
to head the committee.

128
00:06:44,467 --> 00:06:45,968
And that person…

129
00:06:46,552 --> 00:06:49,263
is Prof. Shimazu?

130
00:06:51,265 --> 00:06:53,601
BEST THESIS AWARD

131
00:06:53,684 --> 00:06:54,894
Come in.

132
00:06:56,270 --> 00:06:59,690
NEUROLOGY DEPARTMENT
PROFESSOR MARIKO SHIMAZU

133
00:07:04,528 --> 00:07:05,571
What?

134
00:07:06,364 --> 00:07:09,700
Having fewer possessions
actually implies abundance.

135
00:07:10,493 --> 00:07:14,080
It's a saying left behind by Mies,
a master of modern architecture.

136
00:07:15,081 --> 00:07:16,958
Your room is very sparse.

137
00:07:17,959 --> 00:07:19,126
Thank you.

138
00:07:19,210 --> 00:07:23,339
You're conducting research on Alzheimer's?

139
00:07:24,090 --> 00:07:25,091
Yes.

140
00:07:25,675 --> 00:07:29,637
You are also discussing a licensing
agreement with Eiko Pharmaceutical?

141
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:31,264
About that,

142
00:07:32,181 --> 00:07:34,100
due to the lawsuit, it has been canceled.

143
00:07:34,183 --> 00:07:36,352
But no fraud has been proven yet!

144
00:07:37,186 --> 00:07:38,771
It can't be helped.

145
00:07:38,854 --> 00:07:42,400
It must have been tough,
getting to this point.

146
00:07:43,234 --> 00:07:44,318
Yes.

147
00:07:44,402 --> 00:07:46,112
In our research,

148
00:07:46,195 --> 00:07:50,366
we go through a lot of trial and error
to finally yield a single result.

149
00:07:50,950 --> 00:07:55,329
But, it can only be called complete
once it's used by people.

150
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:58,874
It really is a mind-boggling task.

151
00:07:58,958 --> 00:08:00,293
Just like architecture.

152
00:08:02,628 --> 00:08:05,673
Drawing up plans
and building the structure

153
00:08:05,756 --> 00:08:07,758
isn't the end.

154
00:08:07,842 --> 00:08:11,262
It can only be called complete
once people move in.

155
00:08:13,097 --> 00:08:14,348
And,

156
00:08:15,099 --> 00:08:20,021
if you falsify numbers even a little,
it will become distorted eventually.

157
00:08:20,980 --> 00:08:22,523
-Distorted?
-Yes.

158
00:08:22,607 --> 00:08:24,942
The lies will be exposed.

159
00:08:26,944 --> 00:08:28,321
I'm sorry.

160
00:08:28,404 --> 00:08:32,074
This man is currently
just an architect, so don't mind him.

161
00:08:32,158 --> 00:08:34,493
Well then, I'll take my leave.

162
00:08:34,577 --> 00:08:37,622
I'll leave the rest to you, Aso.

163
00:08:38,247 --> 00:08:39,165
What?

164
00:08:41,584 --> 00:08:44,253
What did he even come here for?

165
00:08:55,931 --> 00:08:57,516
THE FIRST FEMALE RECIPIENT

166
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:00,936
NO MATTER HOW TALL THE WALL
OF DISCRIMINATION, MY RESEARCH GOES ON

167
00:09:02,605 --> 00:09:03,731
EVENT CANCELED

168
00:09:03,814 --> 00:09:05,983
A NEW TREATMENT FOR ALZHEIMER'S
AND ITS PRACTICALITY

169
00:09:06,067 --> 00:09:07,943
EVENT CANCELED

170
00:09:12,740 --> 00:09:13,574
Kureta.

171
00:09:14,158 --> 00:09:16,202
There's something I want you to look into.

172
00:09:19,413 --> 00:09:21,749
It seems the final decision on entry

173
00:09:21,832 --> 00:09:24,043
is made based on
not only the written test,

174
00:09:24,126 --> 00:09:27,672
but also an interview and essay,
as decided by the committee.

175
00:09:27,755 --> 00:09:31,258
About Mr. Kikuta's transport company,
due to the rising fuel costs,

176
00:09:31,342 --> 00:09:33,678
business is rough right now.

177
00:09:33,761 --> 00:09:36,180
Also, his daughter who didn't get in

178
00:09:36,263 --> 00:09:38,891
is currently attending
a private medical school.

179
00:09:38,974 --> 00:09:42,103
Medical school tuition must be no joke.

180
00:09:42,186 --> 00:09:44,980
Did he sue the university for the money?

181
00:09:51,987 --> 00:09:54,990
Regarding your request the other day
for additional funding…

182
00:09:56,325 --> 00:09:57,493
I'm afraid it's a no.

183
00:09:57,576 --> 00:10:00,371
Wait! In that case,
my current research will--

184
00:10:00,454 --> 00:10:01,872
I have no choice!

185
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:04,500
Because of this scandal,

186
00:10:04,583 --> 00:10:09,130
Eiko Pharmaceutical has cut off all
donations and joint research funds.

187
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:13,926
You are sure the exam scandal
will be fine, right?

188
00:10:15,219 --> 00:10:16,137
Yes.

189
00:10:16,220 --> 00:10:19,765
You oversaw the committee
so we would avoid these accusations!

190
00:10:19,849 --> 00:10:20,850
Do your job!

191
00:10:22,226 --> 00:10:23,269
Yes.

192
00:10:25,146 --> 00:10:26,856
Sorry for contacting you so suddenly.

193
00:10:26,939 --> 00:10:28,023
It's fine.

194
00:10:28,733 --> 00:10:30,901
So, what did you want to talk about?

195
00:10:31,485 --> 00:10:32,987
Actually…

196
00:10:33,696 --> 00:10:35,239
TEITO MEDICAL UNIVERSITY
AI MAMIYA

197
00:10:35,322 --> 00:10:38,242
My daughter took the exam
at Teito Medical University too.

198
00:10:38,325 --> 00:10:39,952
But she failed.

199
00:10:40,035 --> 00:10:43,414
She graded herself,
and said it should have been a pass!

200
00:10:43,497 --> 00:10:47,460
Seeing the news, I thought maybe
my daughter was affected too.

201
00:10:47,543 --> 00:10:49,128
I see.

202
00:10:50,254 --> 00:10:52,339
Did they really take points off?

203
00:10:53,466 --> 00:10:54,383
Yes.

204
00:10:54,467 --> 00:10:56,552
But the university is denying it, right?

205
00:11:04,852 --> 00:11:07,605
This was sent to me.

206
00:11:11,984 --> 00:11:13,652
Who is the sender?

207
00:11:15,070 --> 00:11:16,405
I don't know.

208
00:11:16,906 --> 00:11:18,574
May I look inside?

209
00:11:24,914 --> 00:11:27,917
YOUR DAUGHTER WAS SUPPOSED TO HAVE PASSED

210
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,001
What?

211
00:11:33,214 --> 00:11:34,131
This is…

212
00:11:34,215 --> 00:11:36,008
HINATA KIKUTA: 1142
FINAL SCORE: 1132

213
00:11:37,134 --> 00:11:38,385
FEMALE: MINUS 10

214
00:11:38,469 --> 00:11:41,430
They're the exam scores.

215
00:11:46,143 --> 00:11:48,437
SUSPENDED OPERATIONS

216
00:11:48,521 --> 00:11:52,483
We had the data saved in the university
admissions office checked,

217
00:11:52,566 --> 00:11:56,237
but there was no trace of female
applicants' scores being adjusted.

218
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:57,655
I see.

219
00:11:57,738 --> 00:11:58,864
Mr. Kikuta…

220
00:11:58,948 --> 00:12:01,742
On what basis did he sue the school?

221
00:12:01,825 --> 00:12:04,370
It's strange, the timing.

222
00:12:04,954 --> 00:12:06,330
The timing?

223
00:12:07,289 --> 00:12:10,292
True, why now of all times?

224
00:12:10,376 --> 00:12:12,461
The results were out a while ago, right?

225
00:12:26,183 --> 00:12:27,142
Take a look.

226
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:37,069
SCORES
FEMALE - MINUS 10

227
00:12:37,152 --> 00:12:38,153
How did you get it?

228
00:12:39,655 --> 00:12:41,448
Through lies again?

229
00:12:48,622 --> 00:12:49,623
But…

230
00:12:50,332 --> 00:12:55,170
If this is real,
it means the university is lying, right?

231
00:12:58,173 --> 00:13:00,134
I see.

232
00:13:01,010 --> 00:13:04,305
What's in the admissions office
are the final scores.

233
00:13:04,388 --> 00:13:07,850
This is the internal adjustment sheet
created by the committee.

234
00:13:09,602 --> 00:13:12,938
MINUS 10, FAIL

235
00:13:19,612 --> 00:13:21,322
Hey, did you see this?

236
00:13:21,405 --> 00:13:23,407
TEITO UNIVERSITY INVOLVED
IN SCORE-RIGGING SCANDAL

237
00:13:23,490 --> 00:13:25,326
Wait, isn't this crazy?

238
00:13:25,409 --> 00:13:28,287
A FEMALE PROFESSOR, UNFAIRLY FAILING WOMEN
SHE'S THE WORST

239
00:13:28,370 --> 00:13:32,082
I actually liked Prof. Shimazu though.

240
00:13:32,166 --> 00:13:34,918
At this rate, she won't be able
to stay at the university.

241
00:13:35,002 --> 00:13:36,337
Yeah, right?

242
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:37,838
May I sit here?

243
00:13:39,506 --> 00:13:41,592
Oh. Go ahead.

244
00:13:44,053 --> 00:13:45,804
What were you talking about?

245
00:13:46,847 --> 00:13:49,350
Could it be about Prof. Shimazu?

246
00:13:49,433 --> 00:13:53,187
Yeah. Last week,
she was teaching as usual.

247
00:13:53,270 --> 00:13:54,355
Yeah.

248
00:13:54,438 --> 00:13:56,398
She didn't seem the type.

249
00:13:56,982 --> 00:13:59,276
Was there anything different about her?

250
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:02,154
No, we didn't notice anything…

251
00:14:02,237 --> 00:14:03,572
SCORES

252
00:14:13,874 --> 00:14:14,708
What is this?

253
00:14:17,169 --> 00:14:19,838
This was sent to Mr. Kikuta.

254
00:14:21,215 --> 00:14:24,385
It's the internal adjustment sheet
created by the committee, right?

255
00:14:26,762 --> 00:14:29,098
Same format, but I don't think this is it.

256
00:14:33,185 --> 00:14:34,436
But…

257
00:14:35,187 --> 00:14:37,648
this was printed in this lab, wasn't it?

258
00:14:37,731 --> 00:14:38,816
What?

259
00:14:44,029 --> 00:14:45,280
Excuse me.

260
00:14:57,209 --> 00:14:58,127
This line.

261
00:14:59,837 --> 00:15:02,798
It was also on the document
sent to Mr. Kikuta.

262
00:15:05,467 --> 00:15:07,803
No. It wasn't me.

263
00:15:07,886 --> 00:15:09,013
Then who?

264
00:15:09,096 --> 00:15:10,389
I don't know.

265
00:15:12,474 --> 00:15:13,767
I think…

266
00:15:13,851 --> 00:15:16,937
someone is trying to frame me.

267
00:15:17,521 --> 00:15:20,024
Frame you? Why?

268
00:15:22,192 --> 00:15:24,653
Because I am a woman.

269
00:15:26,196 --> 00:15:29,908
Most professors in the university's
medical faculty are men.

270
00:15:29,992 --> 00:15:34,038
"Women will quit anyway
once they get married and have children.

271
00:15:34,747 --> 00:15:37,624
So we can't entrust them
with positions of power."

272
00:15:39,835 --> 00:15:42,588
My research results
were even stolen a few times.

273
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:46,300
This is that kind of world.

274
00:15:46,383 --> 00:15:47,968
I went through that,

275
00:15:48,052 --> 00:15:50,804
and now I would turn around
and ruin other girls' dreams?

276
00:15:51,388 --> 00:15:53,807
It sounds ridiculous, right?

277
00:15:54,641 --> 00:15:55,601
Prof. Shimazu.

278
00:15:57,227 --> 00:15:59,063
Please believe me.

279
00:16:04,318 --> 00:16:08,864
If someone set this up to frame you,
they must have left evidence behind.

280
00:16:09,782 --> 00:16:12,367
I will reveal the truth myself.

281
00:16:18,999 --> 00:16:21,168
I said that, but…

282
00:16:38,769 --> 00:16:41,355
JUSTICE'S FORM

283
00:17:20,769 --> 00:17:25,607
This drawing will reveal all the lies.

284
00:17:34,032 --> 00:17:35,659
Sanno Pharmaceuticals

285
00:17:35,742 --> 00:17:38,787
expressed interest
in a licensing agreement.

286
00:17:38,871 --> 00:17:40,038
What?

287
00:17:40,122 --> 00:17:44,001
Seems they have been interested
in your research for a long time.

288
00:17:44,084 --> 00:17:48,964
After hearing that our agreement
with Eiko fell through, they reached out.

289
00:17:54,344 --> 00:17:55,470
I see.

290
00:17:55,554 --> 00:17:57,723
For the university, it's a lifeboat.

291
00:17:58,265 --> 00:18:01,435
You don't have any problems
with this deal, right?

292
00:18:02,603 --> 00:18:03,812
No.

293
00:18:06,523 --> 00:18:07,733
But…

294
00:18:09,818 --> 00:18:14,114
I would like to choose the lawyer
who will be present when signing.

295
00:18:15,449 --> 00:18:19,494
Yes, some professors
didn't view her favorably.

296
00:18:19,578 --> 00:18:22,623
Especially recently,
it has been all about her.

297
00:18:22,706 --> 00:18:25,542
Feels like the university
was boosting her up too much.

298
00:18:29,963 --> 00:18:33,926
All donations from Eiko Pharmaceutical
were lost because of this scandal.

299
00:18:34,009 --> 00:18:37,304
Because of that,
budgets for other labs got cut as well.

300
00:18:37,387 --> 00:18:39,056
I don't know if it's related to this,

301
00:18:39,139 --> 00:18:43,018
but the other day, Prof. Shimazu
and Prof. Oyamada argued quite a bit.

302
00:18:43,101 --> 00:18:44,853
Prof. Oyamada?

303
00:18:44,937 --> 00:18:47,189
Another professor
in the neurology department.

304
00:18:47,272 --> 00:18:50,192
If I recall, those two used
to be in the same lab long ago.

305
00:18:50,275 --> 00:18:51,109
Yes!

306
00:18:51,193 --> 00:18:55,072
But Prof. Oyamada has always been
unusually harsh toward Prof. Shimazu.

307
00:18:55,906 --> 00:18:58,784
Sorry, when did they have that argument?

308
00:18:58,867 --> 00:19:01,787
NEUROLOGY DEPARTMENT
PROFESSOR TOSHIFUYU OYAMADA

309
00:19:17,928 --> 00:19:20,097
Why is he here?

310
00:19:26,853 --> 00:19:29,356
What did you and the man
from earlier discuss?

311
00:19:31,191 --> 00:19:35,570
He asked me a few questions
about my research.

312
00:19:37,322 --> 00:19:39,574
-And then…
-And then?

313
00:19:40,367 --> 00:19:42,077
We talked about a bridge.

314
00:19:42,577 --> 00:19:43,870
A bridge?

315
00:19:43,954 --> 00:19:48,250
A bridge in London
called the Millennium Bridge.

316
00:19:49,251 --> 00:19:51,044
What about the fraud allegations?

317
00:19:51,128 --> 00:19:52,879
Oh, nothing like that.

318
00:19:52,963 --> 00:19:54,673
What did he even come here for?

319
00:19:54,756 --> 00:19:57,801
Mr. Uramawashi
is an interesting person, isn't he?

320
00:19:57,884 --> 00:19:59,594
No, not at all.

321
00:20:00,429 --> 00:20:03,724
So, what business do you have?

322
00:20:03,807 --> 00:20:06,560
There's something
I want to confirm with you.

323
00:20:06,643 --> 00:20:08,186
Oh, confirm?

324
00:20:09,187 --> 00:20:12,566
I heard you were raising objections
to Prof. Shimazu's research.

325
00:20:12,649 --> 00:20:16,320
Yes, I merely stated my opinion
as a fellow researcher.

326
00:20:16,403 --> 00:20:18,905
Prof. Shimazu was having
great success recently.

327
00:20:18,989 --> 00:20:20,824
Perhaps that irritated you?

328
00:20:20,907 --> 00:20:22,242
No.

329
00:20:22,326 --> 00:20:25,537
I spoke out for her sake,
and nothing else.

330
00:20:28,832 --> 00:20:30,709
By the way, Prof. Oyamada,

331
00:20:31,293 --> 00:20:34,504
you entered Prof. Shimazu's office
when no one was there, right?

332
00:20:35,213 --> 00:20:36,131
What?

333
00:20:39,968 --> 00:20:42,179
<i>It was caught on the security camera.</i>

334
00:20:43,972 --> 00:20:47,142
Prof. Shimazu called for me,

335
00:20:47,225 --> 00:20:51,480
but since she wasn't there,
I waited a little then left.

336
00:20:51,563 --> 00:20:55,901
So, you were in there alone, correct?

337
00:20:56,943 --> 00:20:58,862
Well, yes.

338
00:20:58,945 --> 00:21:02,741
At that time, did you falsify
the scores on the computer

339
00:21:02,824 --> 00:21:04,534
and send them to Mr. Kikuta?

340
00:21:04,618 --> 00:21:05,827
What?

341
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:11,500
What would I get from doing such a thing?

342
00:21:16,004 --> 00:21:17,464
I am not

343
00:21:18,382 --> 00:21:21,843
the kind of person who twists the truth!

344
00:21:32,187 --> 00:21:33,772
Aren't you overdoing it?

345
00:21:34,356 --> 00:21:37,359
It's fine. Muscles don't lie.

346
00:21:37,442 --> 00:21:39,236
You get back what you put in.

347
00:21:39,319 --> 00:21:43,031
It's good to pursue the truth,
but aren't you the university's lawyer?

348
00:21:43,115 --> 00:21:45,409
Isn't this against
your client's interests?

349
00:21:45,492 --> 00:21:47,577
I know that. But…

350
00:21:48,161 --> 00:21:49,496
You can't leave it alone.

351
00:21:51,289 --> 00:21:55,085
For now, I'll do what I can.

352
00:21:55,919 --> 00:21:58,922
But you know, muscles lie too.

353
00:21:59,005 --> 00:21:59,881
What?

354
00:22:00,465 --> 00:22:03,427
If you do it wrong, you'll ruin your body.

355
00:22:04,010 --> 00:22:05,137
Be careful.

356
00:22:12,894 --> 00:22:15,689
Thank you for working so late.

357
00:22:16,523 --> 00:22:18,692
It's just you.

358
00:22:19,276 --> 00:22:22,195
Thanks to a certain someone,
my work has piled up.

359
00:22:22,279 --> 00:22:24,781
Well, business is booming.
Can't really complain.

360
00:22:24,865 --> 00:22:26,283
Yeah.

361
00:22:32,497 --> 00:22:36,084
You used to stay at the company
late back in the day too.

362
00:22:36,168 --> 00:22:38,628
-How nostalgic.
-Yeah.

363
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:46,595
And now, you've gone independent
to build your own castle, Kureta.

364
00:22:47,596 --> 00:22:50,348
It's not a castle, just a shack.

365
00:22:50,974 --> 00:22:53,935
So, how is Ms. Aso doing?

366
00:22:56,813 --> 00:22:59,441
She's doing her best in her own way.

367
00:23:00,817 --> 00:23:03,028
She resembles you.

368
00:23:04,446 --> 00:23:05,822
She's like you.

369
00:23:07,032 --> 00:23:09,826
She also cannot leave
vulnerable people alone.

370
00:23:12,954 --> 00:23:14,539
I'm worried.

371
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,128
A pillar that is too rigid
cannot absorb vibration.

372
00:23:21,421 --> 00:23:23,548
When it breaks, it happens in an instant.

373
00:23:27,719 --> 00:23:31,806
I heard a lawyer came to see you,
asking about the scandal.

374
00:23:32,849 --> 00:23:37,229
About next month's academic conference,
we'll go with another professor.

375
00:23:37,312 --> 00:23:38,438
Please hold on. I--

376
00:23:38,521 --> 00:23:40,690
The university made the decision.

377
00:23:41,316 --> 00:23:43,902
We don't want to cause
unnecessary speculation now.

378
00:23:46,821 --> 00:23:50,742
Please refrain
from public activities for a while.

379
00:23:52,369 --> 00:23:53,828
I understand.

380
00:24:16,059 --> 00:24:17,394
SANNO PHARMACEUTICALS

381
00:24:17,477 --> 00:24:20,230
Sorry for asking you here so suddenly.

382
00:24:20,313 --> 00:24:23,066
Having you come along is reassuring.

383
00:24:23,650 --> 00:24:25,277
I'm happy to help.

384
00:24:25,860 --> 00:24:27,487
Well then, shall we go?

385
00:24:29,322 --> 00:24:30,991
Thank you.

386
00:24:31,700 --> 00:24:32,617
Here.

387
00:24:33,243 --> 00:24:35,036
Thank you for coming!

388
00:24:35,120 --> 00:24:37,580
-Are you from Teito Medical University?
-Yes.

389
00:24:37,664 --> 00:24:39,082
Let me be your guide.

390
00:24:39,165 --> 00:24:40,458
Thank you.

391
00:24:45,714 --> 00:24:47,299
Excuse me.

392
00:24:53,638 --> 00:24:55,932
What a splendid facility.

393
00:24:56,016 --> 00:24:57,767
Thank you.

394
00:25:01,021 --> 00:25:02,439
ALZHEIMER'S NEW TREATMENT R&amp;D TEAM

395
00:25:08,069 --> 00:25:11,031
Right this way, please. Let's go.

396
00:25:12,824 --> 00:25:17,912
By the way,
we've built a new north wing recently.

397
00:25:17,996 --> 00:25:20,707
That building is also very impressive.

398
00:25:21,708 --> 00:25:23,793
Thank you for your time today.

399
00:25:25,045 --> 00:25:27,255
We are aware
of the entry exam allegations.

400
00:25:27,339 --> 00:25:32,761
However, what we care about here
are the research results.

401
00:25:33,553 --> 00:25:36,806
It really was a wonderful thesis.

402
00:25:37,849 --> 00:25:38,850
Thank you.

403
00:25:41,644 --> 00:25:43,146
What do you think?

404
00:25:43,229 --> 00:25:47,025
We believe we have presented
the best possible terms.

405
00:25:47,108 --> 00:25:50,779
It's an honor.
We have no objections at all.

406
00:25:50,862 --> 00:25:52,072
PENALTY CLAUSE

407
00:25:52,155 --> 00:25:54,282
What is this penalty clause?

408
00:25:54,366 --> 00:25:56,117
It applies in the event that

409
00:25:56,201 --> 00:25:58,870
a major issue is found
with the research data.

410
00:25:59,496 --> 00:26:01,456
If there are no issues, it won't matter.

411
00:26:07,045 --> 00:26:10,340
As conditions go,
I think these are pretty good terms.

412
00:26:16,012 --> 00:26:18,932
Please sign the last page.

413
00:26:28,858 --> 00:26:31,820
ADDRESS
REPRESENTATIVE'S NAME

414
00:26:38,243 --> 00:26:39,285
Prof. Shimazu?

415
00:26:43,456 --> 00:26:47,585
I'm sorry. Please pretend
this conversation never happened.

416
00:26:47,669 --> 00:26:48,878
What?

417
00:26:52,674 --> 00:26:53,883
Prof. Shimazu!

418
00:26:58,179 --> 00:27:00,932
Prof. Shimazu! What's wrong?

419
00:27:02,434 --> 00:27:05,437
I'm sorry, I made you come all this way…

420
00:27:06,271 --> 00:27:08,273
Is there a problem with the contract?

421
00:27:09,065 --> 00:27:12,110
No, it's not that.

422
00:27:13,153 --> 00:27:14,737
Then why?

423
00:27:16,614 --> 00:27:17,991
I'm sorry.

424
00:27:22,787 --> 00:27:24,122
Who wants more beer?

425
00:27:24,205 --> 00:27:26,958
Good job. Thank you for today.

426
00:27:27,041 --> 00:27:28,460
Yes, likewise!

427
00:27:28,543 --> 00:27:31,212
-Thank you very much.
-See you!

428
00:27:33,006 --> 00:27:35,216
Man, weren't you nervous, though?

429
00:27:35,300 --> 00:27:37,260
I really was, but…

430
00:27:37,343 --> 00:27:41,514
I'll play a lawyer in my next play,
so this is good practice.

431
00:27:41,598 --> 00:27:44,184
It's a play you were in before, Asuka.

432
00:27:44,267 --> 00:27:45,226
Really? Wow!

433
00:27:45,310 --> 00:27:46,853
Please come see us.

434
00:27:46,936 --> 00:27:48,313
-Of course!
-Come see us!

435
00:27:48,980 --> 00:27:52,525
Still, I can't believe we infiltrated
Sanno Pharmaceuticals.

436
00:27:52,609 --> 00:27:54,527
We can't just build a drug company.

437
00:27:54,611 --> 00:27:57,655
Thanks to that,
expenses were cheaper than usual!

438
00:27:57,739 --> 00:27:59,365
That's the best.

439
00:28:00,867 --> 00:28:02,535
SANNO PHARMACEUTICAL INFILTRATION

440
00:28:05,788 --> 00:28:09,542
I am Mori from Sanno Pharmaceuticals.

441
00:28:10,335 --> 00:28:14,380
I'm calling about Prof. Shimazu's
licensing agreement.

442
00:28:15,590 --> 00:28:18,468
I'm working part-time, starting today.
The name is Nakata.

443
00:28:18,551 --> 00:28:20,553
-Nice to meet you.
-Do your best.

444
00:28:21,221 --> 00:28:22,180
Yes!

445
00:28:22,263 --> 00:28:23,640
Let me be your guide.

446
00:28:23,723 --> 00:28:24,641
Thank you.

447
00:28:30,313 --> 00:28:32,607
What a splendid facility.

448
00:28:37,362 --> 00:28:40,657
Right this way, please. Let's go.

449
00:28:45,620 --> 00:28:48,331
We are aware
of the entry exam allegations.

450
00:28:48,414 --> 00:28:50,834
However, what we care about…

451
00:28:55,630 --> 00:28:56,673
CLEANING
PLEASE WAIT

452
00:28:56,756 --> 00:28:59,133
Please pretend this conversation
never happened.

453
00:29:01,427 --> 00:29:02,846
Prof. Shimazu!

454
00:29:09,686 --> 00:29:12,897
I am truly sorry.
I will contact you again shortly.

455
00:29:13,523 --> 00:29:14,732
Hey!

456
00:29:20,113 --> 00:29:22,198
They left!

457
00:29:25,368 --> 00:29:28,121
Okay. Change into these.

458
00:29:28,204 --> 00:29:29,873
This and this.

459
00:29:29,956 --> 00:29:31,499
-Pass them over.
-Okay.

460
00:29:36,212 --> 00:29:38,006
Huh? What?

461
00:29:38,089 --> 00:29:41,009
Sorry! We just finished cleaning up.

462
00:29:41,092 --> 00:29:42,427
Excuse us!

463
00:29:42,510 --> 00:29:44,262
Excuse us.

464
00:29:45,722 --> 00:29:47,223
That was close, wasn't it?

465
00:29:47,307 --> 00:29:49,893
I was worried
when the real employees came.

466
00:29:49,976 --> 00:29:51,102
-It was close.
-I'm back.

467
00:29:51,185 --> 00:29:54,689
In the nick of time!
You changed so quickly.

468
00:29:54,772 --> 00:29:57,358
I've never seen someone
change clothes so fast.

469
00:29:58,276 --> 00:29:59,986
Sanno Pharmaceuticals?

470
00:30:00,069 --> 00:30:01,487
Oh, that's…

471
00:30:01,571 --> 00:30:04,449
Seems they are building a new wing,
so we had a consultation…

472
00:30:04,532 --> 00:30:06,367
And this is the celebration party.

473
00:30:07,076 --> 00:30:08,411
THE BATTERED LAWYERS

474
00:30:09,162 --> 00:30:11,706
THEATRE TROUPE VISION

475
00:30:11,789 --> 00:30:12,957
Theater troupe!

476
00:30:13,583 --> 00:30:14,918
I can't believe it!

477
00:30:26,095 --> 00:30:27,013
This.

478
00:30:27,096 --> 00:30:29,474
What did you do? Explain yourself!

479
00:30:29,557 --> 00:30:31,809
It was to expose the truth.

480
00:30:31,893 --> 00:30:33,144
The truth?

481
00:30:36,564 --> 00:30:38,107
What were you thinking?

482
00:30:38,900 --> 00:30:40,276
I am very sorry.

483
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:41,736
I'm not asking for an apology.

484
00:30:41,819 --> 00:30:45,698
Do you understand how much this contract
means to the university?

485
00:30:45,782 --> 00:30:47,867
This isn't your problem alone.

486
00:30:47,951 --> 00:30:50,662
It affects the entire future
of our medical faculty.

487
00:30:50,745 --> 00:30:52,580
Why didn't you sign?

488
00:30:52,664 --> 00:30:54,082
Give me a reason!

489
00:31:02,465 --> 00:31:03,883
Prof. Shimazu.

490
00:31:14,268 --> 00:31:15,895
It was you, wasn't it?

491
00:31:16,562 --> 00:31:19,190
The one who made up the grading scandal.

492
00:31:20,817 --> 00:31:23,444
It's not me. Do you have any evidence?

493
00:31:23,528 --> 00:31:27,115
The fact that you turned down the contract
with Sanno Pharmaceuticals.

494
00:31:28,408 --> 00:31:31,995
You rejected such favorable terms
right at the last moment.

495
00:31:33,538 --> 00:31:36,332
A researcher
wouldn't normally turn that down.

496
00:31:37,333 --> 00:31:41,713
The contract going through
would have been inconvenient for you.

497
00:31:43,840 --> 00:31:46,134
I read your thesis.

498
00:31:48,553 --> 00:31:51,472
It was wonderful
with no unnecessary details.

499
00:31:51,556 --> 00:31:52,557
However,

500
00:31:53,474 --> 00:31:55,268
you stripped away too much.

501
00:32:01,733 --> 00:32:02,734
This is…

502
00:32:02,817 --> 00:32:05,820
Clinical data excluded from the paper.

503
00:32:06,988 --> 00:32:10,199
You left only the results
that were convenient for you.

504
00:32:10,283 --> 00:32:14,620
If the contract went through,
the data would be scrutinized,

505
00:32:15,538 --> 00:32:18,291
and the fraud would be exposed.

506
00:32:19,542 --> 00:32:22,045
That's why you didn't sign the contract.

507
00:32:26,049 --> 00:32:27,508
Prof. Shimazu.

508
00:32:28,843 --> 00:32:30,053
Why?

509
00:32:36,934 --> 00:32:38,978
How is the thesis going?

510
00:32:39,812 --> 00:32:41,064
Well…

511
00:32:41,147 --> 00:32:44,025
There are still
minor variations in the data.

512
00:32:44,108 --> 00:32:45,985
I have high expectations for you.

513
00:32:46,069 --> 00:32:48,529
You will become
the face of our institution.

514
00:32:50,364 --> 00:32:51,491
You understand, right?

515
00:32:52,575 --> 00:32:53,659
Yes.

516
00:32:54,285 --> 00:32:56,162
<i>I had no choice but to do this.</i>

517
00:32:57,747 --> 00:32:59,082
In this world,

518
00:33:00,583 --> 00:33:03,503
for a woman to continue her research…

519
00:33:05,379 --> 00:33:09,717
<i>If I don't produce results,</i>
<i>I won't be able to stay here.</i>

520
00:33:10,301 --> 00:33:12,053
As a researcher,

521
00:33:14,055 --> 00:33:16,015
I crossed the line.

522
00:33:16,724 --> 00:33:19,477
The data in this is strange.
You should fix it--

523
00:33:19,560 --> 00:33:21,729
Stop making false accusations.

524
00:33:21,813 --> 00:33:24,273
Are you really going
to leave it like this?

525
00:33:24,357 --> 00:33:27,443
This is my research.

526
00:33:28,778 --> 00:33:30,655
-No, hey.
-Excuse me.

527
00:33:34,117 --> 00:33:38,121
<i>The one who noticed it was Prof. Oyamada.</i>

528
00:33:42,166 --> 00:33:44,752
The fraud would come to light eventually.

529
00:33:45,378 --> 00:33:47,046
To prevent that,

530
00:33:47,130 --> 00:33:49,882
you decided to create
a fake grading scandal.

531
00:33:50,383 --> 00:33:52,426
Our school is strict in scoring.

532
00:33:52,510 --> 00:33:55,012
Then why? Why did she fail?
Please explain!

533
00:33:56,013 --> 00:33:57,932
<i>You targeted Mr. Kikuta.</i>

534
00:33:58,015 --> 00:34:00,017
DEDUCTED POINTS

535
00:34:03,521 --> 00:34:05,982
<i>Created a fake internal adjustment sheet.</i>

536
00:34:15,533 --> 00:34:17,243
<i>Called Prof. Oyamada</i>

537
00:34:17,785 --> 00:34:20,204
<i>and made it</i>
<i>so suspicion would fall on him…</i>

538
00:34:23,624 --> 00:34:24,667
On top of that,

539
00:34:26,210 --> 00:34:27,879
you played the victim.

540
00:34:29,755 --> 00:34:32,175
Why did you take me along?

541
00:34:32,842 --> 00:34:34,677
If you really wanted to hide it,

542
00:34:34,760 --> 00:34:37,221
it would have been better
if I wasn't there.

543
00:34:45,563 --> 00:34:48,316
Because I thought
you would understand, Ms. Aso.

544
00:34:49,859 --> 00:34:51,819
I thought, if my path was wrong,

545
00:34:52,528 --> 00:34:56,073
you would correct me,
like you are doing now.

546
00:35:02,413 --> 00:35:04,415
Since when did you notice?

547
00:35:04,498 --> 00:35:06,542
That she was lying.

548
00:35:06,626 --> 00:35:08,419
When I first met her.

549
00:35:09,086 --> 00:35:14,300
If you falsify numbers even a little,
it will become distorted eventually.

550
00:35:15,259 --> 00:35:16,802
-Distorted?
-Yes.

551
00:35:17,386 --> 00:35:19,138
The lies will be exposed.

552
00:35:20,806 --> 00:35:22,642
Just from that?

553
00:35:22,725 --> 00:35:24,685
How did you know about the thesis?

554
00:35:24,769 --> 00:35:29,148
I heard a rumor that Prof. Oyamada
and Prof. Shimazu were arguing.

555
00:35:30,608 --> 00:35:32,693
So you went to Prof. Oyamada.

556
00:35:32,777 --> 00:35:33,736
Yes.

557
00:35:34,946 --> 00:35:37,323
Then all of that clinical data…

558
00:35:37,990 --> 00:35:41,702
A castle built on lies will surely fall.

559
00:35:45,248 --> 00:35:48,209
<i>This time, there was no grading fraud.</i>

560
00:35:48,292 --> 00:35:51,963
But the university claimed
responsibility for causing confusion,

561
00:35:52,046 --> 00:35:55,258
so they agreed to pay Mr. Kikuta
a settlement fee.

562
00:35:55,341 --> 00:35:56,425
Could it be

563
00:35:56,509 --> 00:36:00,221
you knew Mr. Kikuta was struggling
to pay his daughter's tuition?

564
00:36:00,304 --> 00:36:04,141
But isn't this the best result
for both sides?

565
00:36:04,225 --> 00:36:05,810
I hope so.

566
00:36:05,893 --> 00:36:09,146
Come to think of it,
what happened to your daughter's tuition?

567
00:36:09,230 --> 00:36:11,023
Well, you see…

568
00:36:11,607 --> 00:36:13,609
I won it back at the horse races!

569
00:36:13,693 --> 00:36:16,320
A huge long shot, a real one.
Aren't I amazing?

570
00:36:17,446 --> 00:36:20,283
It's almost time for Mr. Ehara to arrive.
Habu, let's prepare.

571
00:36:20,366 --> 00:36:21,492
Hey!

572
00:36:21,575 --> 00:36:25,246
This is hopeless.
He will never fix his gambling addiction.

573
00:36:27,581 --> 00:36:28,582
<i>Breaking news.</i>

574
00:36:28,666 --> 00:36:30,960
<i>The police have just entered</i>

575
00:36:31,043 --> 00:36:34,547
<i>Teito Medical University and</i>
<i>Eiko Pharmaceuticals to conduct a search.</i>

576
00:36:34,630 --> 00:36:37,967
<i>The university's board chairman</i>
<i>has been suspected of receiving</i>

577
00:36:38,050 --> 00:36:40,344
<i>bribes in exchange for contract favors.</i>

578
00:36:40,428 --> 00:36:42,972
<i>Investigators are looking into</i>
<i>bribery charges.</i>

579
00:36:43,055 --> 00:36:46,934
<i>It seems some professors at the university</i>
<i>acted as whistleblowers…</i>

580
00:36:47,018 --> 00:36:48,561
Could it be…

581
00:36:48,644 --> 00:36:49,895
<i>I am not…</i>

582
00:36:51,105 --> 00:36:54,775
the kind of person who twists the truth!

583
00:36:56,569 --> 00:36:59,196
Was this your goal
from the very beginning?

584
00:36:59,280 --> 00:37:01,282
It has bothered me all along.

585
00:37:01,782 --> 00:37:05,244
The timing of when Eiko Pharmaceutical
terminated the contract.

586
00:37:05,328 --> 00:37:06,579
The timing?

587
00:37:07,288 --> 00:37:08,831
The response was too fast.

588
00:37:11,208 --> 00:37:13,836
It's strange, the timing.

589
00:37:14,462 --> 00:37:15,838
The timing?

590
00:37:16,881 --> 00:37:19,759
True, why now of all times?

591
00:37:19,842 --> 00:37:22,511
The results were out a while ago, right?

592
00:37:23,637 --> 00:37:27,933
You weren't talking about Mr. Kikuta,
but Eiko Pharmaceutical?

593
00:37:28,726 --> 00:37:34,023
A drug company
supporting research is nothing strange.

594
00:37:34,106 --> 00:37:35,399
However,

595
00:37:37,026 --> 00:37:39,320
<i>when the university was sued</i>
<i>for grading fraud,</i>

596
00:37:39,403 --> 00:37:41,822
<i>they cut off all support immediately.</i>

597
00:37:42,323 --> 00:37:47,578
They feared that an investigation
would uncover the illegal funding as well.

598
00:37:48,204 --> 00:37:49,413
Correct.

599
00:37:51,248 --> 00:37:53,667
Some professors had noticed

600
00:37:54,794 --> 00:37:58,714
that there were payments
which went beyond normal support.

601
00:37:59,215 --> 00:38:01,258
However, nobody spoke up.

602
00:38:01,884 --> 00:38:04,512
They feared their own
research budgets would be cut.

603
00:38:04,595 --> 00:38:07,390
Prof. Oyamada was one of them too.

604
00:38:08,099 --> 00:38:12,436
Do you know about
the Millennium Bridge in London?

605
00:38:12,520 --> 00:38:13,354
No.

606
00:38:13,437 --> 00:38:17,817
-It was closed two days after opening.
-Two days?

607
00:38:19,193 --> 00:38:21,862
It swayed much more than expected.

608
00:38:21,946 --> 00:38:23,531
Do you know why?

609
00:38:23,614 --> 00:38:25,783
No idea. Maybe strong winds?

610
00:38:25,866 --> 00:38:28,035
That too, but the answer is…

611
00:38:29,078 --> 00:38:30,579
Resonance.

612
00:38:31,288 --> 00:38:32,248
Or so they say.

613
00:38:32,873 --> 00:38:34,375
Resonance?

614
00:38:34,458 --> 00:38:38,212
In a crowd,
people unconsciously match their pace.

615
00:38:39,004 --> 00:38:43,509
It became a large sway,
and spread throughout the entire bridge.

616
00:38:45,886 --> 00:38:49,682
The bridge was immediately closed,
and they worked to improve it.

617
00:38:49,765 --> 00:38:54,353
As a result,
it became more stable than before.

618
00:38:54,437 --> 00:38:55,646
Really?

619
00:38:55,729 --> 00:38:57,356
An organization is the same.

620
00:38:57,898 --> 00:38:58,899
What?

621
00:39:01,235 --> 00:39:02,486
These…

622
00:39:04,405 --> 00:39:05,781
You really like them, don't you?

623
00:39:05,865 --> 00:39:07,032
Yes.

624
00:39:07,116 --> 00:39:08,951
So do I.

625
00:39:09,535 --> 00:39:10,661
Really?

626
00:39:10,744 --> 00:39:12,580
Eiko Pharmaceutical.

627
00:39:13,289 --> 00:39:14,957
And your university.

628
00:39:16,250 --> 00:39:17,501
You know of their relationship?

629
00:39:18,419 --> 00:39:19,378
Well…

630
00:39:20,171 --> 00:39:24,341
Are you going to keep
turning a blind eye like this?

631
00:39:25,843 --> 00:39:29,054
If someone doesn't speak up,
nothing will change.

632
00:39:30,014 --> 00:39:30,890
No, but--

633
00:39:30,973 --> 00:39:34,185
If one person takes a step forward,

634
00:39:34,768 --> 00:39:37,313
those around them will start to move too.

635
00:39:39,982 --> 00:39:41,150
Won't you cause…

636
00:39:43,652 --> 00:39:44,904
a resonance?

637
00:39:44,987 --> 00:39:46,655
Resonance…

638
00:39:51,285 --> 00:39:53,829
Lies aren't limited to words alone.

639
00:39:53,913 --> 00:39:56,165
They also show in the speed of action.

640
00:39:58,834 --> 00:40:01,378
NEUROLOGY DEPARTMENT
PROFESSOR MARIKO SHIMAZU

641
00:40:41,710 --> 00:40:44,004
Sorry for causing you trouble.

642
00:40:46,215 --> 00:40:48,384
You misunderstood the situation.

643
00:40:49,051 --> 00:40:51,554
I actually respected you.

644
00:40:53,597 --> 00:40:54,848
I don't know anyone else

645
00:40:55,432 --> 00:40:59,228
who took their research
as seriously as you did.

646
00:41:04,441 --> 00:41:06,694
Wasn't today your day off?

647
00:41:07,903 --> 00:41:09,947
I wanted to look into a matter.

648
00:41:11,907 --> 00:41:12,950
<i>That's precisely why</i>

649
00:41:13,742 --> 00:41:16,662
I wanted you to gain success
in a more proper way.

650
00:41:22,376 --> 00:41:25,087
Your research isn't over yet.

651
00:41:26,797 --> 00:41:27,923
Please,

652
00:41:29,174 --> 00:41:31,051
keep going until the end.

653
00:41:35,097 --> 00:41:36,181
I will.

654
00:41:51,947 --> 00:41:53,782
Thank you very much.

655
00:42:08,380 --> 00:42:11,300
There are so many wall colors,
aren't there?

656
00:42:11,383 --> 00:42:12,426
Yes.

657
00:42:12,509 --> 00:42:14,803
There are so many. It's hard to choose.

658
00:42:14,887 --> 00:42:16,722
Living rooms should be white, right?

659
00:42:16,805 --> 00:42:19,016
-White, right?
-White has many shades too.

660
00:42:19,099 --> 00:42:22,061
I've heard that. There are 200, right?

661
00:42:22,144 --> 00:42:23,896
Mika Ahn said that, didn't she?

662
00:42:23,979 --> 00:42:25,356
Stop that!

663
00:42:27,441 --> 00:42:31,945
You have decided that lies
are black-hearted things.

664
00:42:32,613 --> 00:42:35,699
However, reality isn't painted
in such simple colors.

665
00:42:36,450 --> 00:42:37,534
Colors?

666
00:42:38,410 --> 00:42:43,248
Black-hearted lies that hurt people,
bright red lies with no substance.

667
00:42:44,124 --> 00:42:45,959
And lies that save people…

668
00:42:47,044 --> 00:42:48,837
Those are colored gray.

669
00:42:50,839 --> 00:42:53,509
A lawyer's job
is to deal with lies, right?

670
00:42:54,510 --> 00:42:57,054
You need to distinguish their colors.

671
00:42:58,013 --> 00:42:59,306
Distinguish?

672
00:43:02,643 --> 00:43:06,480
Some people can't be saved
through legal means alone.

673
00:43:09,233 --> 00:43:13,445
Even so, the way you do things is wrong.

674
00:43:13,529 --> 00:43:14,822
Excuse me.

675
00:43:19,535 --> 00:43:23,038
Give me a break!
The living room must be white!

676
00:43:23,122 --> 00:43:25,958
But gray is fine too! I'm the one paying!

677
00:43:26,041 --> 00:43:27,626
That again?

678
00:43:27,710 --> 00:43:29,420
Both of you, calm down.

679
00:43:29,503 --> 00:43:32,923
White makes me think of hospitals!
Like I'm sick or something!

680
00:43:33,006 --> 00:43:35,217
Hospitalized? You're so strange!

681
00:43:35,300 --> 00:43:37,177
But putting gray everywhere is weird too!

682
00:43:37,261 --> 00:43:40,013
For the clock, I like white.

683
00:43:40,097 --> 00:43:41,432
Oh, you like white?

684
00:43:41,515 --> 00:43:42,766
-There you go!
-No!

685
00:43:49,648 --> 00:43:51,942
TEITO UNIVERSITY BOARD CHAIRMAN ARRESTED

686
00:43:54,153 --> 00:43:55,070
Yes.

687
00:43:57,698 --> 00:43:59,324
It's about our next meeting.

688
00:43:59,408 --> 00:44:01,618
Seems Mr. Moriki
will also be able to attend.

689
00:44:01,702 --> 00:44:02,828
I see.

690
00:44:02,911 --> 00:44:04,204
Excuse me.

691
00:44:12,880 --> 00:44:16,300
Mr. Ueki, did you watch
the Dodgers game in person?

692
00:44:16,383 --> 00:44:19,219
Yes, my grandchild
wanted to see it no matter what.

693
00:44:19,303 --> 00:44:21,263
A special home run right in front of us!

694
00:44:21,346 --> 00:44:23,682
I'm so jealous!

695
00:44:23,766 --> 00:44:25,976
They're so strong this year too!

696
00:44:26,059 --> 00:44:29,229
The manager's leadership is important.
Right, Mr. Moriki?

697
00:44:30,564 --> 00:44:31,565
Very true.

698
00:44:31,648 --> 00:44:34,401
Man, I'm looking forward
to the World Cup too!

699
00:44:34,485 --> 00:44:36,320
I'm so excited.

700
00:44:36,403 --> 00:44:38,071
I drank too much.

701
00:44:39,239 --> 00:44:41,742
Mr. Moriki, thank you for taking the time.

702
00:44:41,825 --> 00:44:43,786
Same here, thank you.

703
00:44:44,578 --> 00:44:46,830
The Dodgers lost today.

704
00:44:46,914 --> 00:44:48,207
Did they?

705
00:44:49,124 --> 00:44:51,627
I mean, baseball is a team game.

706
00:44:51,710 --> 00:44:54,087
It requires sacrifice bunts,
sacrifice flies…

707
00:44:54,922 --> 00:44:58,800
You must put the team's victory
over individual results.

708
00:44:58,884 --> 00:45:00,802
That is what's necessary.

709
00:45:01,470 --> 00:45:04,223
Only then can you become a strong team.

710
00:45:05,098 --> 00:45:07,434
Don't you think countries are the same?

711
00:45:09,061 --> 00:45:11,980
-Acting as a team will bring strength.
-Yes.

712
00:45:13,398 --> 00:45:14,983
Your car is ready, sir.

713
00:45:15,067 --> 00:45:16,235
I'll be right there.

714
00:45:19,863 --> 00:45:24,618
Mr. Moriki, your firm
has a young female lawyer, right?

715
00:45:25,118 --> 00:45:26,203
Yes.

716
00:45:26,286 --> 00:45:30,582
You must teach her the importance
of playing for the team.

717
00:45:32,668 --> 00:45:34,586
I'm counting on you.

718
00:45:34,670 --> 00:45:35,837
Yes.

719
00:45:46,640 --> 00:45:48,850
<i>-Love?</i>
<i>-No.</i>

720
00:45:49,810 --> 00:45:50,727
<i>Land sharks?</i>

721
00:45:51,311 --> 00:45:52,521
<i>I fell for a scam.</i>

722
00:45:52,604 --> 00:45:54,273
<i>Should I help you with your case?</i>

723
00:45:54,356 --> 00:45:55,774
This is my case.

724
00:45:55,857 --> 00:45:58,151
<i>Definitely don't do</i>
<i>anything strange, okay?</i>

725
00:45:58,235 --> 00:46:01,989
<i>It's too hard for you to handle alone.</i>

726
00:46:02,072 --> 00:46:03,907
<i>Only you can save him, Habu.</i>

727
00:46:04,866 --> 00:46:06,702
<i>Look into his movements for me.</i>

728
00:46:07,202 --> 00:46:08,161
Subtitle translation by: Nix S.

